  "finish": {
    "requirements": [
      {
        "requirement": "Hangs on a wall from keyhole hangers",
        "status": "met",
        "evidence": "Keyhole hangers accessory:0#0,0#3 and accessory:2#0,0#3 sit on the back face (slot 3) of the bottom and top cells. That face is flat all the way up the column, so the rack hangs on two screws already in the wall. The configurator checks fit only, not how much weight the hangers or screws can hold."
      },
      {
        "requirement": "Hooks for small hand tools",
        "status": "met",
        "evidence": "There are 9 hook plates (hex-acc-hook), with 2 hooks each, so 18 hooks in all. They sit on the front face (slot 0) and the two front-angled faces (slots 1 and 5) of all three levels. How much load the hooks can carry depends on the print material, which the configurator doesn't check."
      },
      {
        "requirement": "Rack form / modest size",
        "status": "met",
        "evidence": "The rack has 16 parts: 3 cells, 2 snaps, 2 keyhole hangers and 9 hook plates. check_build is ok and the mesh-collision check is clear."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "I built the rack as a single column of three stacked hex cells at (0,0), levels 0–2, joined by snaps. It has 2 keyhole hangers on the back face and 9 hook plates, which carry 18 hooks. check_build shows no rule errors and the mesh-collision check came back clear. A row of cells side by side can't give one flat back to mount against a wall, so I stacked the cells instead. Their back faces (slot 3) then all line up flat for the hangers. I didn't add any tool props."
  },
